The period from the end of the [[Jade Sea War]] (c. 1390) to the colonial split of 1543 saw the [[Kingdom of Zong]] redirect its strategic, commercial, and political orientation from the continental interior toward the [[Shendan Ocean]] and the [[New World]]. The period was characterised by a post-war economic crisis, the [[Jade Sea Plague]] and the Great Famine of the early 15th century, intense political factionalism within the [[Societal Assembly]], the state-directed programme of oceanic exploration and colonisation that established Zong's first permanent overseas settlements, and the division of the colonial territory into the [[Sinchew]] and [[Louyang]] colonies along the [[Qingjiang]] river.

The pivot was driven by the Darasian trade blockade imposed after the Jade Sea War, which severed Zong's access to the overland and maritime routes connecting eastern [[Fosia]] to [[Illypnia]] and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] world. The blockade contracted the luxury trade that had sustained Zong's economy during the [[Golden Age of Zong|Golden Age]] and forced the kingdom to find alternative sources of wealth.

== Background ==

{{Main|Jade Sea War}}

The [[Jade Sea War]] (c. 1300–1390) was a ninety-year conflict between the [[Chengzhi Dynasty]] of [[Daras]] and the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. Zong successfully repelled the Darasian invasion of [[Bingzhou]] and preserved its territorial integrity, but the military victory came at a strategic cost. Daras, unable to conquer Zong by force, leveraged its control of the continental interior and its alliances with the western Fosian polities to impose a de facto trade blockade on the eastern seaboard. Zong and the eastern maritime states were denied access to the overland and maritime routes that connected eastern Fosia to [[Illypnia]], and the volume of eastern luxury goods reaching western markets contracted sharply.

The blockade did not end with the formal conclusion of the war. Daras maintained the commercial restrictions as a permanent feature of its eastern policy, and the Zong economy — which had been built on the transit trade between the continental interior and the maritime world — was deprived of its primary source of revenue.

== Post-war crisis (c. 1390–1430) ==

=== Economic crisis ===

The trade blockade struck at the foundation of Zong's prosperity. The luxury trade — silks, ceramics, tea, spices, and manufactured goods that flowed from the continental interior through Zong ports to western markets — had been the engine of the kingdom's wealth since the [[Golden Age of Zong|Golden Age]]. The blockade reduced this trade to a fraction of its pre-war volume, and the revenue that sustained the court, the military, and the administrative apparatus collapsed accordingly.

Merchant houses that had depended on the continental trade were ruined or forced into contraction. The shipbuilding industry, which had expanded during the war to meet military demand, lost its primary market. The port cities of the eastern coast entered a prolonged depression. The Zong fiscal system had been designed to extract revenue from the transit trade; when the trade contracted, the system had no alternative base to draw upon. The court was forced to increase taxation on the agrarian interior, creating resentment among the provincial gentry and the peasantry alike.

The blockade also affected the wider eastern Fosian trade network. The [[Sanese archipelago]], which had traded with Zong since the [[Golden Age of Zong|Golden Age]], saw the flow of luxury goods and precious metals through the continental routes to [[Illypnia]] and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] world severely restricted. The [[Minato Shogunate]] of [[Okaiken]], already weakened by the [[Schism of the Dual Suns]] and the internal conflicts that followed, was consumed by its own political fragmentation. As a result, the Sanese archipelago missed the first wave of Shendan Ocean exploration entirely — a consequence that would prove significant when the Sanese belatedly entered the colonial trade in the early 15th century.

=== The Jade Sea Plague and the Great Famine ===

The economic crisis was compounded by two catastrophes in the early 15th century: a trans-Fosian pandemic known as the [[Jade Sea Plague]], and a prolonged famine that devastated the agrarian interior.

The Jade Sea Plague originated in [[Daras]] in the early 1400s and spread across Fosia through the remaining trade and military routes. The disease reached the Jade Peninsula within a few years of its outbreak, carried by merchants, soldiers returning from the continental frontier, and refugees displaced by the war's aftermath. The plague was severe: mortality in the affected regions of Zong is estimated at 10–15% of the population. Western Zong — the agrarian interior closest to the continental frontier and the main corridor for trade and military movement from Daras — was the most severely affected region. The plague's impact was not limited to Zong; it spread across the eastern Fosian world, reaching the [[Sanese archipelago]] and the western Fosian states, and may have contributed to the political instability that consumed [[Okaiken]] during this period.

The famine that followed was not a single event but a cumulative catastrophe that unfolded over two to three decades. The Darasian blockade had eliminated the export markets that sustained the Zong economy. The resulting depression of currency — driven by the lack of exports and the contraction of the money supply — caused a crash in the price of wheat. As wheat prices collapsed, farmers were unable to cover the costs of production, and agricultural investment declined. Fields were abandoned, harvests diminished, and the food supply contracted. The cycle was self-reinforcing: lower prices led to lower production, which led to shortages, which led to price spikes that the impoverished population could not absorb, followed by renewed depression as the shortages temporarily resolved. Over twenty to thirty years, this cyclical collapse produced a major famine that affected the agrarian interior most severely.

The combined impact of the plague and the famine was devastating. The population of the Jade Peninsula contracted significantly, the tax base eroded further, and the court's capacity to respond to the crisis was strained beyond its limits. The famine also produced a social consequence that would prove significant for the colonial project: by the second half of the 15th century, the combination of poverty, food insecurity, and the collapse of the agrarian economy created a large population of people willing to leave the peninsula for the New World. The Zong court would later draw on this displaced population to staff the colonies — not only through the penal transportation that characterised the later Tamsui settlement, but through voluntary emigration by families and individuals who saw the colonial enterprise as an alternative to starvation at home.

=== Political factionalism ===

The economic crisis produced a political crisis within the [[Societal Assembly]]. Two factions emerged:

The Continental Faction (大陸派) argued that Zong must restore the continental trade at any cost. Its members — drawn primarily from the western aristocracy, the established merchant houses, and the diplomatic corps — advocated for a negotiated settlement with Daras, even at the price of significant concessions.

The Maritime Faction (海洋派) argued that the blockade was permanent and that Zong must find new sources of wealth beyond the continental system. Its members — drawn from the eastern coastal provinces, the naval officer corps, and the newer merchant houses that had grown during the war — advocated for a strategic pivot toward the [[Shendan Ocean]] and the development of new trade routes to the east.

The factional struggle dominated the Societal Assembly for four decades. [[Tianyong of Zong|Emperor Tianyong]], who had seen the end of the war, was preoccupied with the immediate economic crisis and deferred the strategic question to the assembly. [[Tian'an of Zong|Emperor Tian'an]], who acceded in 1395, wavered between the two positions, authorising occasional diplomatic overtures to Daras while declining to commit to the full programme of oceanic exploration that the Maritime Faction demanded. The result was a period of strategic paralysis.

=== The House of Yuanlong ===

The [[House of Yuanlong]], which had ruled Zong since the [[Unification of Zong]] and through the Golden Age, continued to hold the throne throughout the crisis. The dynasty's legitimacy was well established, and no rival house mounted a serious challenge for the imperial title. The Yuanlong emperors of the post-war period — Tianyong and Tian'an — were not the commanding figures of the Golden Age. The factional struggle within the Societal Assembly constrained their freedom of action, and the economic crisis limited the resources available for ambitious projects.

The dynasty's survival through the crisis was a testament to the institutional strength of the Zong state. The Societal Assembly, for all its factionalism, provided a framework within which political conflict could be conducted without collapsing into civil war. The imperial succession passed without major disruption, and the administrative apparatus of the state continued to function, albeit at reduced capacity.

== Emperor Tianyang and the eastern reforms (c. 1430–1478) ==

{{Main|Tianyang of Zong}}

[[Tianyang of Zong|Emperor Tianyang]] (天洋, "Heavenly Ocean") acceded to the throne c. 1430 and reigned until his death c. 1478. His reign transformed the Zong state and set the kingdom on the course that would define its history for the next two centuries.

Tianyang was a decisive supporter of the Maritime Faction. His accession marked the end of the strategic paralysis that had characterised the post-war period. The emperor's personal commitment to the maritime programme was unusual among the Yuanlong line — most of his predecessors had been products of the continental-oriented court culture of [[Zhongyuan]] — and it gave the Maritime Faction the imperial backing it needed to overcome the Continental Faction's resistance.

=== The Eastern Reform Programme ===

Tianyang's reform programme had three components:

First, the court formally abandoned the diplomatic effort to restore the continental trade. The embassy to Daras that had been maintained since the end of the war was recalled, and the court declared that Zong would no longer seek the lifting of the blockade through negotiation.

Second, the court restructured the fiscal system to reduce dependence on the transit trade. New taxes were imposed on maritime commerce, shipbuilding, and the eastern port cities, while the agrarian tax burden was partially reduced.

Third, the court authorised a sustained programme of state-directed exploration across the [[Shendan Ocean]]. The court established a dedicated bureau for oceanic exploration, commissioned the construction of new vessels designed for long oceanic voyages, and assigned experienced naval officers to lead the expeditions. The programme was funded by the state and directed by the imperial court, distinguishing it from the merchant-driven exploration that would later characterise other Fosian colonial ventures.

=== The Shendan expeditions ===

The first Shendan expedition departed from the Zong eastern coast in the early 1430s. The fleet, commanded by senior naval officers drawn from the officer corps that had served in the Jade Sea War, sailed eastward across the Shendan Ocean, navigating by the stars and the ocean currents. The distance from the eastern Fosian coast to the western coast of the [[New World]] was comparable to the distance from [[Illypnia]] to the eastern coast of [[Lurandia]] — vast, but navigable with the shipbuilding and navigational knowledge that Zong had developed during the war.

The early expeditions were exploratory: they charted the oceanic routes, identified the prevailing winds and currents, and made initial contact with the western coast of the New World. The reports that returned to Zhongyuan described a vast continent with rich natural resources, indigenous populations engaged in their own maritime and riverine trade networks, and a coastline suitable for the establishment of harbours and trading outposts.

Tianyang's court authorised a series of follow-up expeditions throughout the 1440s and 1450s, each larger and better equipped than the last. The expeditions established seasonal trading contacts with the indigenous peoples of the western coast, exchanging Zong manufactured goods — ceramics, metalwork, textiles — for the tropical hardwoods, precious metals, animal pelts, and agricultural products of the New World.

== Early colonisation (1456–1538) ==

=== Xinhai (1456) ===

{{Main|Xinhai}}

In 1456, the Zong court authorised the establishment of the first permanent settlement on the western coast of the New World. The colony, named [[Xinhai]] (新海, "New Sea"), was founded at a harbour site on the southern coast of the New World mainland.

Xinhai was not a success. The settlement was established in a region with a large and well-organised indigenous population that resisted the Zong presence. The initial colonists — a garrison of soldiers, a contingent of merchants, and a small number of civilian settlers — were unprepared for the scale of indigenous resistance. Within a few years, the colony had been subjected to repeated attacks, its outlying farms and trading posts were destroyed, and its population was confined to the fortified harbour.

By the 1470s, Xinhai had been reduced to a small fortified trading post. The colony was never formally abandoned, but it ceased to function as a viable settlement and served primarily as a resupply station for ships navigating the southern coast. The failure of Xinhai demonstrated that the establishment of permanent colonies on the New World coast required a more careful assessment of local conditions — particularly the strength and disposition of the indigenous populations — than the Zong court had initially undertaken.

=== Anping (1474) ===

{{Main|Anping}}

The lessons of Xinhai informed the selection of the second colony site. In 1474, the Zong court authorised the establishment of [[Anping]] (安平, "Peaceful") at a harbour site further north along the western coast from Xinhai, in what is now modern-day [[Louyang]]. The site had been identified during the exploratory voyages of the 1460s as a region with a more favourable indigenous demographic — the local population was smaller and less centrally organised — and with access to the riverine trade networks of the interior.

Anping succeeded where Xinhai had failed. The colony grew steadily, attracting merchants, farmers, artisans, and their families from the Zong mainland. The indigenous peoples of the region participated in the colonial trade on terms that varied from cooperative to coerced, and the colony developed a plantation economy based on tropical crops that were in demand in Fosian markets.

The colony also became the base for further exploration along the northwestern coast of [[Lurandia]]. Expeditions dispatched from Anping charted the coastline to the northwest and established a series of smaller trading outposts and resupply stations.

=== The Zong Edict of 1500 ===

As the colonial enterprise expanded, the question of labour became increasingly pressing. The plantations of Anping required a workforce that the voluntary settlers and the small flow of penal transportees could not supply. The colonial administration, the merchant houses, and the plantation owners all sought additional sources of labour, and the most readily available source was the indigenous population of the New World.

The enslavement of indigenous peoples was practised from the earliest years of the colonial enterprise. Colonial expeditions captured indigenous communities during military campaigns, and the plantation economy of Anping relied on coerced indigenous labour to work the tropical crop fields. The practice was not unique to Zong — the Illypnian colonial powers that would later establish themselves on the eastern coast of the New World employed similar systems — but the scale of the practice in the Zong colonies grew as the plantation economy expanded.

The issue came to the attention of the imperial court through the reports of colonial administrators and the petitions of Tianist clerics, who argued that the enslavement of indigenous peoples was inconsistent with the ethical framework of the Zong state. In 1500, [[Tianjing of Zong|Emperor Tianjing]] issued the Zong Edict of 1500, which banned the practice of slavery on the Zong mainland. The edict prohibited the enslavement of any person within the territory of the Kingdom of Zong and criminalised the transport of enslaved persons into the kingdom.

The edict was a significant moral and legal statement, but its application to the overseas colonies was ambiguous. The colonial territories were administered as separate jurisdictions, and the edict did not explicitly extend to Sinchew, Louyang, or the smaller outposts. The ambiguity was not resolved by the court, which was reluctant to impose restrictions on the colonial economy that might jeopardise the flow of revenue from the Shendan trade. The result was a legal grey area in which slavery was banned on the mainland but tolerated, or at least not actively prohibited, in the overseas colonies — a contradiction that would persist for over a century and that would later enable the [[Secret Trade]] in enslaved peoples from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]].

=== Northern Lurandian exploration ===

The decades between the founding of Anping (1474) and the establishment of Tamsui (1538) were a period of sustained coastal exploration and incremental colonial expansion. Zong expeditions, operating from Anping and the smaller outposts along the coast, surveyed the northwestern coastline of Lurandia, mapping the harbours, rivers, and indigenous settlements. The exploration was methodical and state-directed, consistent with the programme established under Tianyang and continued by his successors [[Tianjing of Zong|Tianjing]] (1478–1521) and [[Tiancheng of Zong|Tiancheng]] (1521–1560).

The northwestern coast of Lurandia proved more hospitable than the southern coast where Xinhai had been established. The indigenous populations were smaller and less centrally organised, the harbours were more accessible, and the climate — while still tropical — was less hostile to Zong settlers. The court authorised the establishment of a network of trading outposts that extended the Zong presence along the coast.

The expansion was not without conflict. Indigenous communities resisted the Zong presence in some areas, and the competition for trade access and territory produced periodic violence. The Zong response was typically a combination of military demonstration and diplomatic accommodation — the court had learned from the Xinhai experience that sustained conflict at the end of a long supply line was unsustainable.

=== Founding of Tamsui (1538) ===

{{Main|Tamsui}}

In 1538, the Zong court authorised the establishment of a new colony at [[Tamsui]], on the northwestern coast of Lurandia, northwest of Anping. The site had been identified during the coastal surveys of the preceding decades as a location with an excellent natural harbour, access to the riverine trade networks of the interior, and a defensible position that could be fortified against both indigenous resistance and potential competition from other Fosian powers.

Tamsui was initially administered as a colonial city within the [[Louyang]] colony, the southern territory governed from [[Anping]]. The colony was a penal settlement and plantation — the court transported convicts, debtors, and other undesirables to Tamsui to serve as labour for the plantation economy, alongside a smaller population of voluntary settlers drawn by the prospect of commercial opportunity. The climate was hot, humid, and disease-ridden, and the colony's early years were marked by high mortality. Those who survived, however, found opportunities for wealth that were unavailable on the Zong mainland, and Tamsui developed a reputation as a place where fortunes were made or lives were lost.

=== Tamsui Insurgency and the colonial split (1543) ===

{{Main|Tamsui Insurgency}}

Tamsui's distance from the colonial capital at Anping created a structural problem that became increasingly acute as the northwestern settlement grew. The colonial administration in Louyang was oriented toward the south-western settlements — the older, more established, and more profitable plantation districts around Anping — and the northwestern frontier around Tamsui received comparatively little investment, military support, or administrative attention.

The grievance came to a head in 1543 with the outbreak of the [[Tamsui Insurgency]], a revolt by the colonial leadership of Tamsui against the authority of the Louyang administration. The insurgents — drawn from the merchant elite, the plantation owners, and the military garrison of the northwestern settlement — seized control of the colonial administration in Tamsui and declared their refusal to submit to Anping's governance until the Zong court addressed their grievances.

The Zong court, confronted with the prospect of a colonial civil war, chose to resolve the dispute by dividing the colonial territory. The [[Qingjiang]] (清江, "Clear River") in northern Lurandia, which emptied into the sea at the Hook of Tianhe, was designated as the boundary between two separate colonial administrations. The territory northwest of the Qingjiang, centred on Tamsui, became the [[Sinchew]] colony. The territory southeast of the river, centred on Anping, remained the [[Louyang]] colony. Each colony was granted its own governor, its own administrative apparatus, and its own direct relationship with the metropolitan court in Zhongyuan.

The split resolved the immediate crisis and established a dual-colony structure that would govern the Zong presence in the New World for the next century. Louyang remained the wealthier and more established of the two, with its plantation economy and its closer commercial ties to the Zong mainland, while Sinchew developed as a frontier society — harsher, more militarised, and more autonomous in its governance.

== Society and culture ==

The Shendan pivot reshaped the domestic life, religious landscape, and cultural imagination of Zong. The discovery of the New World, the establishment of colonial trade, and the increasing orientation toward the oceanic world produced changes that penetrated far beyond the court and the Societal Assembly.

=== Spread of Baiduism and elite unease ===

{{Main|Baiduism|Baiduist Expulsion from Zong}}

The post-war period saw the accelerating spread of [[Baiduism]] across the eastern coast of Zong. The religion, which had been introduced through the maritime trade routes and adopted primarily by the seafaring communities of the eastern ports, found a new audience among the merchant houses that had grown wealthy on the Shendan trade. The Ocean Crossing (海渡) — the Baiduist teaching that the hundred crossings were like a voyage across the sea, requiring both individual effort and the grace of favorable winds — resonated with a population whose livelihoods now depended on the Shendan Ocean.

The Baiduist monasteries of the eastern coast functioned as commercial institutions — banks, warehouses, and trade intermediaries — as well as religious ones. Their networks extended across the Shendan Ocean to the New World colonies and through the continental routes to the Baiduist heartland in Daras and Shansi. The wealth and institutional autonomy of these monasteries made them competitors to the Tianist establishment, and their foreign connections — particularly their ties to Darasian Baiduist institutions — made them objects of suspicion in the post-blockade political climate.

The Tianist elite viewed the growth of Baiduism with increasing alarm. The emperor's legitimacy was ordained by the Tianist church; the Societal Assembly was staffed by Tianist officials; the fiscal and military apparatus of the state was embedded within a Tianist framework. The Continental Faction argued that Baiduism was a Darasian import that served the interests of the blockade state, and that the religion's growth on the eastern coast was evidence of continental subversion. Provincial officials in the western interior imposed restrictions on Baiduist temple construction and monastic ordination. The stage was being set for the confrontation that would culminate in the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong]] of 1606.

=== The New World and the cultural imagination ===

The discovery of the New World transformed the cultural imagination of Zong. For centuries, the kingdom's mental geography had been oriented toward the continental interior. The Shendan pivot reoriented this geography toward the east, and the reports that returned from the oceanic expeditions filled the Zong cultural imagination with a new world of possibilities.

The court chroniclers of the Tianyang era recorded the first reports from the Shendan expeditions with a mixture of wonder and anxiety. The New World was described as a vast continent of dense forests, great rivers, and peoples whose customs and appearances were entirely unfamiliar to Zong observers. The reports circulated beyond the court through travellers' tales — some reliable, many embellished — that spread through the port cities, creating a popular image of the New World as a land of wealth and danger. The Xinhai disaster reinforced the danger.

The cultural impact was most visible in the eastern port cities, where the Shendan trade was concentrated. The architecture of the ports began to incorporate motifs drawn from New World designs — tropical hardwoods, unfamiliar patterns, and decorative elements adapted from indigenous crafts. The literature of the period, particularly the popular verse forms of the eastern coast, took the oceanic voyage and the New World as recurring themes.

=== Spices, goods, and the colonial trade ===

The Shendan trade introduced a range of new goods into Zong domestic life. The most visible were the spices — pepper, cinnamon, nutmeg, and other tropical products that had previously been available only through the continental trade routes at far higher prices. The blockade had made these spices scarce and expensive in Zong; the Shendan trade restored them at prices that were within reach of a broader population.

Other colonial goods followed: tropical hardwoods used in furniture and construction, dyes and pigments in vivid colours unfamiliar to Zong artisans, cotton and other fibres for the textile industry, and medicinal plants that were adopted by both Tianist and Baiduist healers. The plantation economy of Anping and later Tamsui produced sugar, tobacco, and other cash crops that were exported to the Zong mainland and, through the Shendan trade networks, to the wider Fosian world.

The flow of goods was not one-directional. Zong manufactured products — ceramics, metalwork, silk textiles, and lacquerware — were exported to the colonies, where they commanded high prices and were traded to indigenous communities in exchange for local products. The colonial market created new demand for Zong craftsmanship, and the eastern port cities that had been devastated by the blockade found a new source of commercial vitality in the Shendan trade.

=== Indigenous visitation ===

The Shendan trade also brought indigenous peoples of the New World to Zong. The earliest visitors were trade emissaries — representatives of indigenous communities who accompanied the returning expeditions to establish diplomatic and commercial relationships with the Zong authorities. These emissaries were received at the eastern ports and, in some cases, escorted to the capital at Zhongyuan, where they were presented to the emperor and the Societal Assembly.

The court treated the emissaries as representatives of foreign polities — analogous to the envoys of the [[Jyunhap]] or the [[Three Luoyan States]] — and extended to them the ritual hospitality appropriate to their perceived status. As the colonial trade expanded, the flow of indigenous visitors increased. Some came as merchants, establishing themselves in the eastern ports as intermediaries. Others came as labourers, recruited — or in some cases coerced — into service on the colonial plantations and subsequently transported to the Zong mainland. A small number came as religious seekers, drawn to the Tianist and Baiduist institutions of the peninsula by curiosity or devotion.

The indigenous presence in the eastern ports, though never large, produced cultural effects. Indigenous crafts, clothing, and ornaments entered the markets, where they were collected as curiosities or adapted into Zong decorative traditions. Indigenous foods and preparation methods were introduced to the port cities, adding new flavours to a cuisine that had previously been shaped entirely by Fosian ingredients.

=== Sanese mercenaries and the Sengoku Export ===

{{Main|Okaiken|Sengoku Period}}

The political fragmentation of the Sanese archipelago during the [[Sengoku Period]] (1467–1582) produced a flow of thousands of disenfranchised Sanese warriors across the Shendan Ocean. The collapse of the [[Minato Shogunate]] and the provincial civil wars that followed left large numbers of [[samurai]] without masters, without income, and without prospects in the archipelago. For these men — the [[rōnin]] and the dispossessed retainers of defeated clans — the Zong colonies in the New World offered an alternative to starvation or banditry at home.

The flow of Sanese mercenaries to the New World, sometimes called the Sengoku Export, began in the mid-16th century as the colonial competition between Zong and the emerging Illypnian powers intensified. The Zong colonial authorities, particularly in the [[Sinchew]] colony, welcomed the Sanese warriors as a source of military manpower. The samurai were disciplined, experienced, and accustomed to a level of violence that colonial warfare demanded, and they could be hired at rates that were modest by Zong standards but attractive to men who had nothing left in the archipelago.

The Sanese mercenaries served as garrison troops in the colonial forts, as expeditionary forces in campaigns against indigenous resistance, and as security details for the merchant houses that operated the plantation economy. Some were employed directly by the Zong colonial administration; others were hired by private interests — plantation owners, trading companies, and merchant consortiums.

The presence of Sanese mercenaries in the Zong colonies had cultural as well as military consequences. The warriors brought with them the traditions of the Sanese martial culture and the religious practices of [[Iron Baiduism]], the stern Zong-imported school of Baiduist philosophy that had taken root among the northern warrior elite of Okaiken. The intersection of Sanese martial culture with the Zong colonial environment produced a distinctive frontier society in Sinchew.

The Sengoku Export also shaped the perceptions of the Sanese that would later be held by the Illypnian colonial powers. When the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] expedition of [[Berenguer de Montcada]] encountered the Fosian colonies during the [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564, the Sanese mercenaries they faced — disciplined, lethal, and utterly foreign — left an impression that would influence Illypnian attitudes toward both the Sanese and the Zong colonial enterprise for decades to come.

== Legacy ==

The period reshaped Zong's position in the Fosian world. The kingdom that emerged from the post-war crisis was poorer in continental trade but richer in oceanic reach, and its colonial empire — the network of settlements, trading outposts, and administrative centres that stretched across the western coast of the New World — would become the foundation of its power for the next two centuries.

The pivot also set the stage for the later conflicts that would define Zong's colonial period. The [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564, when the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] expedition of [[Berenguer de Montcada]] discovered the Fosian colonies on the western coast, introduced the Illypnian colonial powers as competitors for the New World. The [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong]] of 1606, which transported over a million Zong Baiduists to Sinchew, transformed the colony from a penal settlement into a major population centre.

